# 4's read-only git, one rule per subcommand per configured root.
#
# The root has to be written into the rule. An allow rule matches
# everything before its first wildcard literally, and Claude Code warns
# about and ignores an allow rule whose wildcard precedes the
# subcommand — so `git --no-optional-locks -C * status:*`, which is the
# rule anyone would reach for first, matches nothing at all. Checked.
# Pinning the subcommand is what makes the grant read-only: no
