# Open the prompt, unlink it, and hand it over as stdin — never as an
# argv string (docs/tasks/0023, pass-4 review finding 1).
#
# `exec claude -p "$prompt"` put the whole prompt in one argument, and
# Linux caps a single argument at MAX_ARG_STRLEN: 32 pages, 131072
# bytes on a 4 KiB-page machine. That was unreachable while the prompt
# held a human-typed request. It is not now: since docs/tasks/0023 the
# packet embeds every prior result body verbatim, each carrying a
# fenced unified diff, under an explicit no-truncation policy — four or
# five turns of real model-authored diffs cross the cap. Past it,
# `exec` fails E2BIG, bash exits 126 saying only "Argument list too
# long", and the turn is recorded `outcome: failed`. Because the
# resuming turn's claim already spent the answer, that errand can never
# auto-resume again: an errand that got long enough would die
# permanently, with the journal blaming the tenant.
#
# Four properties this shape keeps, each of which the obvious
# alternatives break:
#
#   * The temp file is gone before the handoff. `exec 3<` opens it,
#     `rm` unlinks the path, and the fd keeps the inode alive with no
#     name in the filesystem — strictly better than before, where the
#     resident's verbatim words also sat in `claude`'s argv, readable
#     from /proc/<pid>/cmdline for as long as the turn ran. A trap
#     still cannot do this job, for the reason that comment always
#     gave: this script execs, so no EXIT trap of its own ever fires.
#   * The exit code still propagates exactly. `exec` replaces this
#     process, so what `run_worker_turn` waits on is the tenant itself
#     and its status is the tenant's — unchanged. A pipeline would have
#     needed explicit `PIPESTATUS` handling to say the same thing.
#   * 0021's process-group handling is untouched, and this is the
#     reason `exec` was worth preserving rather than piping. `castle
#     work` spawns this script with start_new_session=True and captures
#     the pgid at spawn; `exec` means the tenant is that same pid, in
#     that same group, so the timeout kill and the straggler sweep
#     reach exactly what they always did. A `cat file | claude` shape
#     would have forked a subshell and a second process into the group
#     for no gain.
#   * It does not collide with this script's own stdin. `castle work`
#     writes the packet there and `errand_records="$(cat)"` consumed it
#     to EOF long before this line; redirecting fd 0 from the prompt
#     now reuses a descriptor nothing is still reading.
exec 3< "$prompt_file"
rm -f "$prompt_file"

exec claude "${claude_argv[@]}" <&3 3<&-
